#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179597 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/id #uuid "e4893301-34d3-5f63-9d1c-715588d98d40" :question/math-academy-id "e-9531" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}) \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." :question/worked-solution "The notation $\\displaystyle\\oint$ is used to remind us that we're dealing with a line integral over a *closed* curve.\n\nWe will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos{t}, \\qquad\ny = \\sin{t}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,j=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t)+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179598 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "a448227d-9bf3-54dd-8639-cbdc56a5a403" :question/math-academy-id "e-9532" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the curve $y^3 = x-5$ traversed from the point $A(5,0)$ to the point $B(6,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Making $x$ the subject of the equation, we have\n\n$$\ny^3 = x-5\n\\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad\nx = y^3 + 5.\n$$\n\nSo, we can parametrize the curve using\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =({t}^{3}+5)\\,i+t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t^3+5, \\qquad\ny = t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+({t}^{3}+5)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+5)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+({t}^{3}+5)\\,j)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =t\\cdot 3{t}^{2}+({t}^{3}+5)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{t}^{3}+{t}^{3}+5 \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{3}+5\\,dt \\\\  & ={[{t}^{4}+5t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+5-0 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179599 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/id #uuid "9152629f-47a8-5a71-9bd9-3f5205788f5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9533" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf F(x, y) =(1-y)^2\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(0, 2)$ to the point $B(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$\\mathbf{a} = 2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{i}.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =2j+t[i-2j] \\\\  & =t\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t = 1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad\n\ty = 2-2t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(1-y)^{2}\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =(1-(2-2t))^{2}\\,i+t\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-1)^{2}\\,i+t\\,j \\\\  & =(4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\,i+t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-2t)j \\\\  & =i-2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\,i+t\\,j)\\cdot (i-2j) \\\\  & =(4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\cdot (1)+(t)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}-6t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{2}-6t+1\\,dt \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}-3+1)-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6434469-1749-5df8-9000-f01893b94638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\sin t + 4t\\sin (t^{2})dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177742} {:answer/id #uuid "f39fe168-22ec-5e69-aafa-66a8710a1fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\sin t + 4t\\sin (t^{2} - 1)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177743} {:answer/id #uuid "08e97c23-852d-5a73-b52f-061e3a0c40e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\cos t + 4t\\sin (2t^{2} - 1)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177744} {:answer/id #uuid "28257dfc-7abd-5d10-8977-76f400b99f65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\cos t + 4t\\sin (2t^{2} - 1)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177745} {:answer/id #uuid "33b189d2-9b33-585b-8051-e116d98891cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\sin t + 4t\\cos (t^{2})dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177746}] :answer-field/id #uuid "281b8c1b-0aa5-57dc-b60a-9cd72b4bdf28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177741}] :question/id #uuid "d4e0663c-1824-56f9-b923-60eb7cd3a338" :question/math-academy-id "q-124703" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = \\cos xi + \\sin yj$ and $C$ is the curve $y = 2x^{2} - 1$ traversed from the point $A(1, 1)$ to the point $B(2, 7)$?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t) = ti + (2t^{2} - 1)j$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3c563c9-8c9b-55da-b8c2-070042347609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177760} {:answer/id #uuid "0dde43ea-dd20-502b-9156-72d9581da6f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177761} {:answer/id #uuid "19255a63-77b4-5533-9b49-2a20815502be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177762} {:answer/id #uuid "4a62c57f-bb46-535a-bd54-25ff210b61f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177763} {:answer/id #uuid "6480e3a7-53e0-513b-b505-7959ab4767ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177764}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d6a48da5-21ec-5631-9f71-f7840fbba2c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177759}] :question/id #uuid "2a8909b1-1a48-5c56-ac06-8b954196fc3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124936" :question/problem "Evaluate $∮_(C)F(r) \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = yi - xj$ and $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8aabdf92-da1c-5e64-ac6c-480f0d28e8c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\cos t + \\sin tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178577} {:answer/id #uuid "bec36fd3-5344-5079-a033-edfba9aa7748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\sqrt{\\cos t - \\sin t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178578} {:answer/id #uuid "1afbccc7-23ae-546c-839a-4ff6c35cbf74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2} - \\sin te^{\\cos t} + \\cos te^{-2\\sin t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178579} {:answer/id #uuid "1d619724-123a-5296-9a7f-7c865611174f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\cos te^{\\cos t} + \\sin te^{-2\\sin t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178580} {:answer/id #uuid "66bc2858-ffab-56a4-a655-0c6a9fd10fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}e^{\\cos t} + e^{-2\\sin t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178581}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a80c467a-5d65-5bd2-8003-74460bf3619d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178576}] :question/id #uuid "e19e4fc2-d0da-5e51-a13e-b5b2b6e03a48" :question/math-academy-id "q-39341" :question/problem "![](images/48/4807f92defb4202359a32ca046073cd138db396a0975164e42b96126ac0cc591.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = e^{x}i + e^{-2y}j$ and $C$ is the arc of a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin traversed counterclockwise from the point $(1, 0)$ to the point $(0, 1)$?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t) = \\cos ti + \\sin tj$.*"}]}
